course: dictionary
lesson: 507
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the words around them show you which one is right. Assemble is one of these words. Ask yourself: who is doing it, and what is happening?
explain: «Pupils assemble in the hall for assembly.» Here assemble means to come together in one place as a group. «Furniture often comes flat-packed for you to assemble.» Here it means to put the parts together to build something.
explain: In old writing the word could mean to collect things together. The word list shows «assembled» = gathered. When you meet a word with two meanings, put each meaning into the sentence and see which one makes sense.
fact: Assemble is a verb: assemble, assembles, assembled, assembling.
fact: «Assemble» means to come together in one place as a group.
fact: «Assemble» also means to put the parts of something together to build it.
fact: «Assemble» can mean to collect things together, and «assembled» can mean gathered.
fact: The assembly point is the place where everyone gathers during a fire drill.
exercise: «Pupils assemble in the hall every morning.» What does assemble mean here? | To build something from parts | To sing loudly | To run away quickly | *To come together in one place as a group
exercise: «Furniture often comes flat-packed for you to assemble.» What does assemble mean here? | To wrap something in paper | *To put its parts together to build it | To come together as a group | To sell it for less money
exercise: In the old word list, «assembled» = gathered. What does assembled mean? | Frightened | Built from parts | Hidden away | *Gathered together
test: «Smartphones are assembled from parts made by many companies.» What does assembled mean here? | Came together in a crowd | *Put together from parts to build something | Thrown out | Hidden away
test: In a fire drill, what is the assembly point? | The way to the bus stop | *The place where everyone gathers as a group | The place where the fire starts | The door to the playground
exam: Which sentence uses assemble to mean put parts together to build something? | Assemble quickly when you hear the alarm | Pupils assemble in the hall for assembly | Assemble in the playground when you hear the bell | *Easy to assemble, with all the screws in one box
